course: adult-life
lesson: 35
title: Closed doors, whispers and secrets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sometimes grown-ups close a door, lower their voices or spell out words so children won't understand. It feels as if something big is being hidden, but most of the time it is about money, plans, a relative's problem, a disagreement, or even a nice surprise.
explain: Grown-ups talk privately first so they can be honest, work things out calmly, and then tell you once, clearly and gently, instead of giving you half-finished worry that might turn out to be nothing.
explain: Scene: in Vancouver, Mum puts a hand on Dad's arm: «Not in front of the kids.» The kitchen door closes and the voices go low. Your little sister presses her ear to the door and hears only «hospital» and «can't afford», and gets scared. Later Mum explains: Grandpa needs a small operation and they were planning the trip to visit him.
explain: Good secrets are about timing, like a surprise party that ends in a happy moment. A secret someone says you must never tell, especially about being touched or hurt, is never OK, and you should tell a trusted adult.
fact: «Dad and I need to talk» means a private, serious talk; it does not mean you are in trouble.
fact: A closed kitchen door and low voices mean grown-ups are discussing something they are not ready to share.
fact: Listening at the door usually gives you only scary half-pieces; asking «Is everything OK?» later works much better.
fact: «Not in front of the kids» stops a disagreement or upsetting news happening where children can hear it.
fact: Grown-ups spell words like P-A-R-K so young children will not get excited before a plan is certain.
fact: Some news, like an illness, a job change or a move, is kept back until the grown-ups know more and can explain it properly.
fact: A surprise is a happy secret that will be shared soon, and everyone is glad when it comes out.
fact: A secret you are told never to tell, especially about touching or being hurt, is not OK, and you should tell a trusted adult.
fact: Parents do not always agree, and they usually talk it over and come back with one rule.
fact: Asking the other parent after one says no rarely ends well.
fact: Grown-ups keep friends' secrets too, so whispering on the phone is often about someone else's private problem.
fact: «Go and ask your father» can mean the parent is unsure, wants to decide together, or has no energy left right now.
exercise: Mum, at the lounge door: «Dad and I need to talk.» Does it mean you're in trouble? | No, they want a private, serious talk | Yes, you are about to be punished | Yes, it always means terrible news | No, it means they want to watch TV
exercise: The kitchen door closes and the voices go low. What is going on? | They're discussing something they're not ready to share | They're planning to move house in secret | They're hiding from you in the kitchen | They're playing a game of whispers
exercise: Mum puts a hand on Dad's arm: «Not in front of the kids.» Why? | To keep a grown-up talk away from children | Because the kids are being too noisy | Because Dad was about to give them sweets before their tea | To stop Dad telling a funny joke
exercise: Dad spells «P-A-R-K» over your head. Why? | So little ones don't get excited before it's certain | Because he can't remember how to say it | Because "park" is a secret word that only grown-ups are allowed to say | To test whether you can spell yet
exercise: You can spell P-A-R-K. What is the kind thing to do? | Keep it to yourself so your little brother isn't let down | Shout «PARK!» so everyone knows | Tell Dad he spelled it wrong and should practise his spelling more | Run and get your shoes on now
exercise: Overheard: «We won't tell the children yet.» Why do grown-ups wait? | To tell you once, clearly, when they know more | Because children are never told anything | Because they think you can't understand | Because they forgot to tell you first
exercise: Mum: «It's a surprise, so don't tell Gran about the party.» Is that an OK secret? | Yes, it's a happy secret that ends soon | No, all secrets from grown-ups are bad | No, you must tell Gran straight away | Yes, and you must keep it forever
exercise: Someone says «Never tell anyone» about being touched or hurt. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult | Keep it secret forever | Only tell your little sister | Forget about it and say nothing
exercise: Dad: «Mum says no sweets, but I said yes earlier.» What usually happens next? | They talk it over and agree on one rule | You get double sweets from both of them | Mum and Dad stop talking for a week | You decide which parent is right
exercise: Mum says no. Should you go and ask Dad without telling him? | No, that rarely ends well | Yes, it's the best way to get a yes | Yes, Dad always says yes anyway | No, you should ask Gran instead
exercise: Mum turns away on the phone and whispers. What's most likely? | She's talking about someone else's private problem | She's saying something bad about you to a friend on the phone | She's ordering your birthday present from a shop | She has lost her voice from a cold
exercise: Mum, tired of arguing: «Go and ask your father.» What can it mean? | She's unsure or wants them to decide together | She has decided the answer is a definite yes | She never wants to talk to you again | Dad is the only one in the house who makes rules
exercise: You go to ask Dad after Mum sent you. What's the honest way? | Tell him Mum sent you to ask him | Say Mum already said yes | Don't mention Mum at all | Ask him while he's on the phone
exercise: Why is listening at the door a bad idea? | You only hear scary half-pieces | You might hear too much good news | Doors are too thick to hear anything | Grown-ups always talk about the weather
exercise: What's a better way than listening at the door? | Ask later, «Is everything OK?» | Hide under the table to listen | Read Mum's phone messages | Ask your friend to listen for you
exercise: What makes a surprise different from a bad secret? | A surprise ends soon in a happy moment | A surprise is always about presents | A bad secret is just a longer surprise | A surprise must be kept forever
test: Your sister says «When they shut the door, it means they're getting divorced.» Put her right. | It's usually money, plans or other people's news | Yes, a shut door always means a divorce | It means they're angry with you two | It means someone has died in the family
test: Overheard through the door: «...the hospital...» Then silence. What's the best thing to do? | Ask Mum calmly later if everything's OK | Panic and tell everyone someone is dying | Keep listening until you hear more | Pretend you heard nothing and worry alone
test: Uncle: «Keep this between us, kiddo — a new puppy for your mum's birthday Saturday!» Is it OK to keep? | Yes, it's a happy surprise that ends on Saturday | No, every secret from a grown-up is bad | No, you must tell Mum this minute | Yes, and you must never tell her ever
test: An older boy says «If you tell anyone what happened, you'll be in big trouble.» You feel scared. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep the secret so you're safe | Only tell it to your diary | Wait until you're grown up
test: Dad: «Chocolate before tea? Fine by me.» Mum: «I said NO.» Dad: «Right, sorry, we'll sort it.» What happens next? | They sort it out between them and agree one rule | You get to choose whose rule you like better each time | You get chocolate because Dad said first | They stop speaking to each other forever
test: Dad, to Mum: «Shall we get I-C-E C-R-E-A-M after?» Your little brother can't spell yet. What's going on? | They don't want him excited before it's sure | They are practising their spelling | They want to keep the ice cream for themselves | Ice cream is a grown-ups-only treat
test: You asked Mum for a sleepover, she said «ask your father», and you tell Dad «Mum said it's fine». What happens? | It backfires when they talk to each other | You get the sleepover with no problem at all, and a takeaway too | Dad gives you two sleepovers instead | Mum forgets she ever said anything
test: Mum hangs up after whispering on the phone. «Aunt Kate's having a tough time, it's hers to share, not mine.» What does it show? | Grown-ups keep friends' secrets too | Mum is hiding something about you | Aunt Kate is cross with your family | Mum doesn't trust anyone at all
test: Gossip at the gate: «They haven't told the kids yet, she's moving jobs to Glasgow.» Why might the parents wait? | To tell the children once, when they know more | Because children never need to know | Because they want the children to find out by gossip | Because the move is a surprise party
test: What would happen if grown-ups told children every worry the moment it began? | Children would worry about things that turn out to be nothing | Children would never feel worried about anything at all ever again | Grown-ups would stop having any worries | Nothing, it would make no difference
test: Cousin: «Grown-ups whispering on the phone means they're talking about you.» Is that right? | No, it's usually someone else's private business | Yes, they're always talking about the kids and what they did wrong | Yes, and it means you're in trouble | No, grown-ups only whisper about money
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the declaration «Dad and I need to talk»? | It denotes a private and serious conversation between the parents | It denotes that a child is to be punished forthwith for misconduct | It denotes invariably that a calamity has occurred | It denotes an intention to watch television together
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain news is withheld from children for a period. | To permit its communication once, clearly and gently, when more is known | To prevent the children from ever learning of the matter in any form whatsoever | To punish the children for previous misconduct | To ensure the news is disclosed through gossip
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a secret relating to being touched or hurt. | It must be disclosed to a trusted adult | It must be retained indefinitely as instructed | It may be disclosed solely to a younger sibling | It must be disclosed only upon reaching adulthood
exam: It is given that one parent has refused a request and the child is directed to the other parent. Determine the most appropriate conduct. | Inform the second parent that the first parent sent the child | Inform the second parent that permission was already granted | Conceal entirely the involvement of the first parent | Abandon the request and address neither parent again
